WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:07.830
- Hello.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:11.130
Welcome to "The Benjamin and
Barbara Zucker Lecture Series"

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:14.490
at Yale's Program for the
Study of Antisemitism.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.140
My name is Alice Kaplan.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.870
I'm the director of the
Whitney Humanities Center

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:22.200
and a member of the
Advisory Board of YPSA.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.190
I'm delighted to welcome Julia
Elsky, Associate Professor

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:29.100
in the Department of Modern
Languages and Literatures

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.890
at Loyola University Chicago.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.880
It's a special pleasure to
welcome Julia back to Yale.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:39.930
She completed her Ph.D. in French in 2014,

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.840
and since then, her
accomplishments in the twin areas

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.800
of Jewish studies and French
studies have been remarkable.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.030
In 2020, she published
"Writing Occupation:

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.540
Jewish Emigre Voices in Wartime France"

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.150
with Stanford University Press's series

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.370
on Jewish history and culture,

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:03.720
and you'll see a link
to the book under chats.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:07.380
This book studies how Jewish
emigre writers re-examined

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:08.760
both their Jewishness

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.320
and their place as authors

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.410
writing and publishing or not publishing

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:16.440
in France during the occupation.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:19.200
Her talk this afternoon
is drawn, in large part,

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.520
from research conducted

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:24.450
at the United States
Holocaust Memorial Museum.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:25.980
Julia has published articles

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.930
on World War II and its legacy

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:30.682
as well as on Jewish identity in France

00:01:30.682 --> 00:01:33.877
in "PMLA," "Yale French Studies,"

00:01:33.877 --> 00:01:36.750
"La Nouvelle Revue
francaise," "Archives juives,"

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.310
and other journals.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:41.010
She's the recipient of
grants and fellowships

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:42.960
too numerous to name.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:43.920
She's contributed

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.890
to the Holocaust Museum's
digital collection

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:49.320
of objects of memory

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.060
called "Experiencing History."

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.710
We're also putting that on the chat.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:57.060
It's really a fascinating
digital experience,

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.490
clicking on that link.

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:02.340
Please, keep an eye out for
two of her current projects,

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.230
both of them co-authored,

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.900
one called "Undesirables in France:

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.330
German-Jewish Women,
Artists, and intellectuals

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.040
during the Holocaust"

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.460
and, still in the planning
stages, a museum exhibit

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:18.870
on the flight of the avant-garde
through wartime Marseille.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.990
And finally, Julia Elsky's
new book project is

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.540
about the origins of the
theater of the absurd

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.310
during the Second World War

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:31.310
with a focus on Ionesco's inner
war and wartime experiences.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.410
Today, she will speak to us

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:38.970
about a Jewish poetics of
exile, Benjamin Fondane,

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:42.450
and Jewish emigre authors
in occupied France.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:47.450
Please, submit questions,
as we go, in the Q and A.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.740
Julia will speak for about 40 minutes.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.173
And then, we'll open it up for discussion.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.940
- Hello, I hope this is working now

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.240
with me speaking (laughs).

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:02.100
I would like to thank
YPSA for inviting me,

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.910
especially Alice Kaplan
and Maurie Samuels.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:08.040
It's really a true honor to speak at Yale,

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.670
where I did my Ph.D. in French
and where I began the project

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:16.260
from which I'm drawing my
research for my talk today

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.540
with the generous guidance of Alice Kaplan

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:21.030
and much support from Maurice Samuels.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:22.860
And this work would really not be possible

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.013
without your intellectual and
personal generosity, Alice.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.160
In my talk today,

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.800
I will first discuss
the historical context

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:36.300
of Jewish Eastern European
immigrants to France

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.890
in the interwar period

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:39.330
and then their experiences

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:42.000
under the Nazi occupation of France.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.350
I'll then turn to the question

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:46.320
of how two Jewish writers
changed their approaches

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:47.760
to writing in French

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.273
in response to the persecution
of the Jews in France.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:54.720
The title of my talk focused on Fondane,

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.160
but actually, I really should've said

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:59.313
it's about Fondane and one
other writer, Jean Malaquais.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:01.193
First, I'll talk

00:04:01.193 --> 00:04:04.170
about the Polish-born
novelist Jean Malaquais

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:07.470
to think about the question of
anti-Semitic representations

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.200
of Jewish voice and speech.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:11.970
And then, second, I will address

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:16.080
the Romanian-born poet and
philosopher Benjamin Fondane

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:19.050
and his representation of a sense of exile

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:20.433
under the occupation.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:25.770
One of the main points
that I wanna make today

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:28.650
is that Jewish immigrant
writers did not stop thinking

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:30.330
about their place in France

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:35.100
and as French writers at the
moment of the June 1940 defeat.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:39.330
The start of the occupation
did not usher in their silence.

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:42.060
In fact, this period has
often been overlooked

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.000
in studies of European Francophonie

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.820
and even in studies of
Jewish writers in France.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.920
And by European Francophonie,

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.610
I'm referring to
non-French European writers

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:55.020
who wrote in French,

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:57.363
whether or not they immigrated to France.

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:00.510
But I argue that the
period of the occupation

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:02.130
is a crucial period of study,

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.290
and it was precisely under the occupation

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.590
that these authors addressed
the choice to write in French

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:10.053
and faced anti-Semitism in new ways.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:15.900
Jean Malaquais and Benjamin
Fondane were among a number

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.380
of writers who came to
France in their teens and 20s

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:21.210
in the interwar period,

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.280
and I wanna share...

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.280
Sorry. These are some of
the writers I've studied.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:29.610
They came to France and
mostly settled in Paris

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:32.310
to become French writers in a country

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:36.600
that represented universalism
and Jewish civil rights.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:39.903
They also wanted to be part
of a major literary center.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.080
The period after World War I

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.390
saw the largest wave of Jewish immigration

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:46.770
in French history,

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:50.100
one that radically
transformed the landscape

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:51.363
of French Jewry,

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:56.040
and I have some facts and figures here.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.020
But by 1940,

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:01.527
of approximately 330,000 Jews in France,

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:05.070
140,000 were immigrants
without French citizenship,

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:08.190
and 55,000 were naturalized.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:13.190
And you could see, by 1926,
6% of the population of France

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:16.440
were immigrants, Jewish and non-Jewish.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:19.860
And I noted an important
law, from August 10th, 1927,

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:24.000
that eased requirements
for naturalization,

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.573
and this will become important
again under the occupation.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:30.600
I'll now give a little bit of context

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.420
about the experience of the
occupation by Jewish immigrants

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:35.430
from Eastern Europe, in particular,

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:38.070
and focusing on two aspects:

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:41.283
the June 1940 civilian flight from Paris;

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.210
the lights just went off here, I'm sorry;

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:46.863
well, okay, they turned back on;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.890
sorry, focusing on the June
1940 civilian flight from Paris,

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:55.680
called the Exode or Exodus,

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.110
and then life under the occupation,

00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:01.323
living with the threat of
arrest and deportation.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:04.050
In June 1940,

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.780
just before the German
forces entered Paris

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:09.240
and just before France signed an armistice

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.283
that divided the country in half,

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:14.790
into a southern and a northern zone,

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:17.580
there was a massive
civilian flight southward,

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:19.890
called the Exode, as I mentioned.

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:21.690
Paris's population dropped

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:23.370
from almost three million people

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:27.030
to between 700,000 and one million people.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:30.660
In a matter of weeks, the
major city was emptied.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:34.030
Scholars estimate that the
roads of France were flooded

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.110
with around six million people,

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:40.440
or 1/6 of the population, the country,

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:44.970
and two million Belgians
and Luxembourgers.

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:47.880
Irene Nemirovsky famously
depicted this flight

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:50.700
in her novel "Suite francaise"
that was rediscovered

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:52.380
in the early 2000s,

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:56.130
and Benjamin Fondane also represented it.

00:07:56.130 --> 00:08:00.300
Emigre authors were in a
unique position in June 1940,

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:02.880
as they were fleeing in a country

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:04.533
to which they had immigrated.

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:08.010
Shannon Fogg has written extensively

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:09.932
about how the Exodus forced
people to rely even more

00:08:09.932 --> 00:08:14.932
on individual social structures
and networks for survival,

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:17.520
and emigre writers, indeed, often relied

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.410
on their intellectual networks.

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:21.240
And this was not always possible

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:23.553
for other Jewish emigres in France.

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:27.180
As people doubly displaced from home,

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:29.550
emigre writers were particularly in tune

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:31.380
with the notion of exile,

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:34.440
and this perhaps explains why
they wrote, during the war,

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.870
about the events of June 1940

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:40.590
in far greater numbers than
their French-born colleagues.

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:43.590
Aside from emigre
authors' fictional texts,

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:47.190
there are surprisingly few
literary depictions of the Exodus

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:48.990
that were written during the war,

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:51.753
although there do exist
many journalistic accounts.

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:55.140
Whether in the occupied or southern zones,

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:56.640
Jewish immigrants were subject

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.850
to anti-Semitic and xenophobic legislation

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:02.820
and were threatened with
internment and deportation.

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:06.000
As historian Patrick Weil argues,

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.350
there was no clear division

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:10.200
between anti-Semitic and xenophobic laws

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:11.850
under the occupation.

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:13.530
Rather, they were interwoven

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:15.333
and even competed with each other.

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:18.630
From the beginning of the occupation,

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:21.120
the vulnerable status of Jewish refugees

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:24.750
and recently naturalized
Jewish-French citizens

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:29.460
was at the center of legal
changes and government action.

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:31.780
The law of August 10th, 1927,

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:34.710
that, I mentioned earlier, eased

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:36.780
requirements for naturalization

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:38.820
was used as a negative symbol

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:42.150
of the supposed degradation of France,

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:45.600
and it became a vehicle
for denying immigrants

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:47.970
the right to belong as French citizens.

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:52.741
Over 15,000 French people
were denaturalized,

00:09:52.741 --> 00:09:57.180
and 110,000 Algerian Jews
lost their citizenship.

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:00.180
Vichy passed anti-Semitic Jewish statutes

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:02.667
in October 1940 and June 1941.

00:10:02.667 --> 00:10:06.090
And I indicate toward
the bottom of this slide,

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:09.168
a day after the first Jewish statutes,

00:10:09.168 --> 00:10:11.040
(indistinct) law was passed

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:13.503
that allowed for the
internment of foreign Jews.

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:18.360
After the defeat, foreign
Jews were the first

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:19.730
to be rounded up in Paris

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:22.860
in the spring and summer of 1941.

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:25.740
Approximately 3,000 foreign-born Jews died

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:27.270
in French internment camps

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:28.923
early in the occupation.

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:31.530
With the collaboration of Vichy,

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:34.650
Jewish foreign refugees
in French internment camps

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:35.850
were deported eastward

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:39.780
in the first convoys from the
southern zone in August 1942

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:41.730
to concentration camps.

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:43.230
I'm giving these numbers just to indicate

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:46.320
that they were often the first wave

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:49.710
of victims of deportations.

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:54.240
The very tragically
famous Vel d'Hiv roundup

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:55.710
of July 1942

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:59.520
targeted almost 30,000 people

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:03.210
who were stateless or
unnaturalized Jewish immigrants

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:05.820
from Germany, Austria,
Poland, Czechoslovakia,

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:07.053
and the Soviet Union.

00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:09.810
By the end of the war,

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:13.980
only 3% of Jewish people
deported from France returned.

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:17.043
75% of Jews in France survived.

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:22.170
And this is a number

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:24.180
that's often in the news,

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:25.830
and it's important to think about

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:28.440
and also to be careful about it.

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:31.440
But when you break it down,
90% of French Jews survived,

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:33.873
compared to 60% of foreign Jews.

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:37.290
I'm gonna stop sharing for a moment,

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:38.740
even though I'll share again.

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:42.180
Under the occupation,

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:45.600
numerous Jewish immigrant authors
responded to anti-Semitism

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:46.680
in new ways,

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:49.890
especially with regard
to the French language.

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:51.720
These authors conceived

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:53.580
of their position as Jewish immigrants

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:56.250
through writing itself during the war,

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:59.340
and just as they were rejected from France

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:00.750
as immigrants and as Jews,

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:04.200
or in reaction to this double rejection

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:05.553
as immigrants and as Jews.

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:10.470
Why do I focus on the
question of French language?

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:12.990
First, the language question is imbricated

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:15.990
in the politics of
assimilation and integration,

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:18.390
including in citizenship law

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:20.880
and across the political spectrum.

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:23.580
This was the case during
the interwar period

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:25.350
and under Vichy.

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:28.500
In the interwar period, as
Nick Underwood has shown

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:31.140
in his exciting new book, "Yiddish Paris,"

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:33.660
French language lessons
were an important part

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:36.870
of the cultural activities
of Yiddish organizations.

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:39.750
Looking at the topic of
language and integration

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:41.880
from a totally different angle,

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:46.080
the demographer Georges
Mauco published a study

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:49.890
of immigration in France in 1932

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:53.100
based on racial classifications.

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:55.590
The scholar Patrick Weil,
who I mentioned earlier,

00:12:55.590 --> 00:12:57.630
has done a lot of work on Mauco.

00:12:57.630 --> 00:13:01.890
Mauco; that's M-A-U-C-O;
refers to language

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:05.820
as one of the main, or the
major, factors of assimilation.

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:08.850
He argues, based on a so-called fact,

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:11.820
that Slavic immigrants cannot
master the French language,

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:15.390
which is obviously not true,
but his argument is that.

00:13:15.390 --> 00:13:17.310
He argues that they are
never going to be able

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:20.370
to assimilate, and his
conclusions were actually accepted

00:13:20.370 --> 00:13:23.973
on the left and on the far right
during the interwar period.

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:27.750
Mauco would be considered
one of the major specialists

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:29.340
on immigration under Vichy

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:31.830
when he wrote anti-Semitic texts.

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:34.080
One month after the
start of the occupation,

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:35.550
the Ministry of Justice sought

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:37.980
to create a new nationality code,

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:40.620
and in this code, a criteria would be

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:42.900
speaking and understanding French.

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:45.420
And it should be noted
that Vichy never succeeded

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:47.523
in creating their new nationality code.

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:52.020
The second reason for focusing on language

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:54.630
is that many Jewish
emigre writers responded

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:57.300
in their literature and
in their writing in French

00:13:57.300 --> 00:13:58.533
to a specific current

00:13:58.533 --> 00:14:03.120
in the xenophobic press of the far right.

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:04.230
The far right featured

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:06.930
anti-Semitic and
xenophobic representations

00:14:06.930 --> 00:14:08.880
of Jewish language and accent

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:10.620
as the evidence of the impossibility

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:14.190
of including Jews and Jewish
immigrants in the nation.

00:14:14.190 --> 00:14:16.200
This attitude goes back

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:19.800
to "La France juive," or "Jewish France,"

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:23.730
written by the journalist
Edouard Drumont in 1886,

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:25.743
and it continues in the occupation.

00:14:26.610 --> 00:14:28.637
A foundational text of
French anti-Semitism

00:14:28.637 --> 00:14:30.450
in the 19th century,

00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:33.420
Drumont's "La France juive" declares

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:35.160
that Jews cannot really speak French,

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:38.413
because they cannot truly
think like French people.

00:14:38.413 --> 00:14:41.640
Drumont even claims that one
can recognize a Jewish person

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:44.583
from their pronunciation
and their accent in French.

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:47.790
Drumont, I'm using his terms.

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:50.070
He says there's no difference
between an Alsatian Jew

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:51.720
and an immigrant from the Levant.

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:56.070
A particularly interesting example

00:14:56.070 --> 00:14:58.260
of an article from the occupation

00:14:58.260 --> 00:15:02.613
draws on Jewish language
among refugees in Marseille.

00:15:02.613 --> 00:15:03.570
And as I mentioned,

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:06.900
there's this major civilian
flight from Paris southward,

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:08.730
and Marseille became a new center

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:10.980
of intellectual life during the war.

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:13.380
French and refugee
artists from around Europe

00:15:13.380 --> 00:15:16.140
congregated there as they
waited for visas to leave.

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:18.000
And I'm excited to be working more

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:21.640
on this aspect of Marseille's history

00:15:23.928 --> 00:15:26.100
with my colleague Alyssa Shapiro.

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:28.020
From the viewpoint of the far right,

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:31.280
the panorama of accents
in 1940s Marseille...

00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:34.710
There's lot of writing
about the cafes that resound

00:15:34.710 --> 00:15:37.140
with the accents of refugees.

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:38.820
You find that representation

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:41.583
both among refugees themselves
and in the far right.

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:45.360
But from the point of
view of the far right,

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:47.280
these accents that you could hear

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:50.073
were the sign of racial
corruption of the French city.

00:15:51.030 --> 00:15:53.673
Let me share again.

00:15:57.563 --> 00:16:01.757
'Kay, the anti-Semitic
collaborationist writer

00:16:01.757 --> 00:16:04.710
Lucien Rebatet describes the city

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:08.130
in his article "Marseille la
juive," or "Marseille the Jew,"

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:09.000
which he published

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:11.637
in the collaborationist
newspaper "Je suis partout."

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:14.880
Marseille appeared to him to be dominated

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:17.670
by Jewish immigrants from
Eastern and central Europe.

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:21.150
One of the defining features
of the new Jewish presence

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:22.170
is their language,

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:24.783
and Rebatet generalizes it as Yiddish,

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:27.480
which isn't necessarily the case,

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:30.210
but the way he writes about
it is that it's all Yiddish.

00:16:30.210 --> 00:16:32.280
And for Rebatet,

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:34.140
Jewish language is a violent assault

00:16:34.140 --> 00:16:36.690
on the calm evenings of the terrace,

00:16:36.690 --> 00:16:38.580
of the cafe (speaks in French),

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:40.207
and I have some quotes here:

00:16:40.207 --> 00:16:42.720
"Cocktail hour is now Jew hour".

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:44.730
When he hears a Jewish person talking,

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:47.610
he says, "He spits out
some words in Yiddish."

00:16:47.610 --> 00:16:51.000
As for other Jewish people
frequenting the cafes,

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:53.550
he writes, "Yiddish
explodes on their tongues.

00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:56.547
Their claw-like hands wave
around as high as their ears."

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:01.590
Rebatet writes that the Polish
and Czech Jews in the cafes

00:17:01.590 --> 00:17:05.670
seem to be conventional
but far from virile,

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:08.340
in his representation,
middle-aged Bourgeois men,

00:17:08.340 --> 00:17:10.380
but their language gives them away.

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:13.830
He says, "They're comfortably
bourgeois in their gray suits

00:17:13.830 --> 00:17:17.160
but, with their sinister
mugs of featherless owls,

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:20.550
largely past their 50s,
gibbering in their vile Yiddish,

00:17:20.550 --> 00:17:23.580
which resembles German mangled by a sidi."

00:17:23.580 --> 00:17:27.243
So not only does Rebatet
single out Jewish difference.

00:17:28.746 --> 00:17:30.030
It's a really interesting quote.

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:32.220
He conflates the Jew and the Arab.

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:35.970
Sidi is a derogatory
term for a North African,

00:17:35.970 --> 00:17:38.883
an appropriation of the Arab honorific.

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:43.143
So the Jew is like an Arab
trying to speak German.

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:47.047
As Sandrine Sanos writes in her book,

00:17:47.047 --> 00:17:48.270
"The Aesthetics of Hate:

00:17:48.270 --> 00:17:51.030
Far-Right Intellectuals,
Anti-Semitism, and Gender"

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:52.623
in 1930s France,

00:17:53.520 --> 00:17:54.937
and I'm quoting from her,

00:17:54.937 --> 00:17:57.090
"Colonial racism and anti-Semitism

00:17:57.090 --> 00:17:58.950
were imbricated, rather than parallel,

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:01.200
in this far-right discourse.

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:04.980
Rebatet's article is,
in fact, a prime example

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:06.660
of what Sanos describes

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:09.720
as 'Je suis partout's'
approach in the 1930s

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:12.150
that"; again, I'm quoting her; "promoted

00:18:12.150 --> 00:18:15.210
a racialized Frenchness
whose boundaries were mapped

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:17.580
through the simultaneous
and necessary subjection

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:20.400
of colonized subjects
abroad in the empire,

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:22.920
which allowed the denunciation
and verbal persecution

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:24.990
of French Jews within the nation."

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:27.630
And these boundaries of the
French nation were conceived of

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:30.840
in terms of anxieties about masculinity

00:18:30.840 --> 00:18:34.323
and the exclusion based on
racial and sexual difference.

00:18:35.310 --> 00:18:36.983
In his article on Marseille,

00:18:36.983 --> 00:18:41.040
Rebatet hears the male
Jewish refugee accents,

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:44.880
or what he reduces to Yiddish accents,

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:47.340
on the cafe (speaks in
French) of Marseille

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:50.190
as a threat to the
boundaries of pure French

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:51.330
and thus of a nation

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:54.360
whose greatness must be
upheld through empire.

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:55.230
It is a sign

00:18:55.230 --> 00:19:00.180
of an unchangeable and
inassimilable Jewish otherness,

00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:02.550
and in the context of the war,

00:19:02.550 --> 00:19:05.670
I wanna mention that accents
are particularly fraught;

00:19:05.670 --> 00:19:07.710
for example, speaking in
what would be thought of

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:09.180
as a foreign Jewish accent

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:12.310
would expose you as a refugee

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:16.143
and thus expose you to
the threat of deportation.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:19.800
Racist and xenophobic
ideas about Jewish accents

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:22.410
were not new to the
period of the occupation.

00:19:22.410 --> 00:19:25.320
Sander Gilman argues that the stereotype

00:19:25.320 --> 00:19:30.150
of, quote, "The Jew who
sounds Jewish," or too Jewish,

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:33.480
stands for the Jew who
unmasks himself or herself

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:36.180
as, quote, "possessing all languages

00:19:36.180 --> 00:19:39.090
or no language of his or her own

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:42.087
and, as such, is inherently
different and corrupt."

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:48.277
In a xenophobic and anti-Semitic
book published in 1939,

00:19:48.277 --> 00:19:50.070
"La grande invasion,"

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:53.227
the author, Saint-Germain,
entitles a chapter

00:19:53.227 --> 00:19:54.720
"Le 'bedide gommerze'"

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:57.333
as I note here on the PowerPoint.

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:02.490
This chapter's about how Jews
and foreigners are taking over

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:04.410
small shops in France,

00:20:04.410 --> 00:20:07.803
and this title is a
caricature of Jewish voice.

00:20:09.930 --> 00:20:14.760
This is like the way, in
French, you have this mocking

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:16.290
of the Jewish voice,

00:20:16.290 --> 00:20:20.400
and we see the same, the B
and the G, in other texts,

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:22.170
including in Balzac's novels

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:24.810
as Maurice Samuels has written about.

00:20:24.810 --> 00:20:27.450
And it's important to think
about these continuities

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:31.470
in the discourses of Jewish
voice and anti-Semitism

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:34.350
and anti-Semitic
representations of Jewish voice

00:20:34.350 --> 00:20:38.070
to take into account how
the writers respond to this

00:20:38.070 --> 00:20:40.320
in the interwar period and during the war,

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:42.450
because I don't wanna just bracket off

00:20:42.450 --> 00:20:45.660
the years of the occupation, 1940 to 1944,

00:20:45.660 --> 00:20:47.850
as if it's this closed-off time.

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:50.760
But it's, rather, part
of a continuous time

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:52.413
in the lives of these writers.

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:56.253
I'll finally get to Jean Malaquais,

00:20:58.260 --> 00:21:01.440
and I'll maybe stop sharing
my screen for a moment.

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:04.440
Jean Malaquais is the pen
name of Wladimir Malacki,

00:21:04.440 --> 00:21:05.850
a Polish-born novelist

00:21:05.850 --> 00:21:08.250
who came to France in the 1920s.

00:21:08.250 --> 00:21:09.990
While he was working as a stock boy

00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:13.650
in Les Halles, the food halls,

00:21:13.650 --> 00:21:14.730
he began to write,

00:21:14.730 --> 00:21:16.830
and under Andre Gide's mentorship,

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:18.150
he wrote "Les javanaise,"

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:21.150
which is translated as
"The Men from Nowhere,"

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:23.010
about miners in France,

00:21:23.010 --> 00:21:27.960
miners as in people
who work in mines, E-R.

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:32.670
He won the 1939 Prix Renaudot
literary award for this novel,

00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:35.220
and after the French
defeat, Malaquais fled

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:36.690
to the southern zone.

00:21:36.690 --> 00:21:39.330
He survived with the help
of writers and editors

00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:42.270
who were now based in the south of France.

00:21:42.270 --> 00:21:43.860
And eventually, he moved to Marseille

00:21:43.860 --> 00:21:46.980
and joined its world of
intellectual refugees.

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:49.110
During the war, he began to engage

00:21:49.110 --> 00:21:51.870
with the idea of Jewish
language and Jewish characters

00:21:51.870 --> 00:21:56.127
in his novel "Planete sans
visa," or "World without Visa."

00:21:57.180 --> 00:21:59.970
And so this is an example
of a shift in writing.

00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:03.543
He really begins to write about
Jewish voice during the war.

00:22:04.470 --> 00:22:07.650
He wrote "World without Visa"
over the course of five years,

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:11.400
from 1942, while he was waiting
for a visa in Marseille,

00:22:11.400 --> 00:22:15.030
to 1947, when he was living in Mexico.

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:17.040
And he was able to get out of France

00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:22.040
with the aid Varian Fry's famous
emergency rescue committee.

00:22:22.740 --> 00:22:25.350
His novel presents a world of refugees

00:22:25.350 --> 00:22:28.650
in the tense atmosphere

00:22:28.650 --> 00:22:31.380
of Marseille under Vichy rule

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:34.620
prior to the Nazi invasion
of the southern zone

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:36.363
in November 1942.

00:22:37.920 --> 00:22:42.150
In "World without Visa's"
multilingual society of refugees,

00:22:42.150 --> 00:22:45.840
Malaquais reclaims and
valorizes what was mocked

00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:47.790
in the anti-Semitic press,

00:22:47.790 --> 00:22:51.910
both the use of Yiddish
and Jewish refugee accents

00:22:52.860 --> 00:22:56.820
as I showed in the article
"Marseille la juive."

00:22:56.820 --> 00:22:59.490
Malaquais's wartime diaries indicate

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:01.680
that even when he was in Mexico,

00:23:01.680 --> 00:23:03.900
he was consulting "Je suis partout,"

00:23:03.900 --> 00:23:07.800
the newspaper that published
Lucien Rebatet's article,

00:23:07.800 --> 00:23:10.230
and it's reasonable to
conclude that he was familiar

00:23:10.230 --> 00:23:12.630
with this kind of discourse.

00:23:12.630 --> 00:23:16.950
The first third of Malaquais's
almost-700-page novel

00:23:16.950 --> 00:23:19.720
recounts the increasingly tense days

00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:24.000
leading up to a large
roundup of Jews in Marseille,

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:27.390
culminating in the arrest
of the Krantz family,

00:23:27.390 --> 00:23:29.880
so it's a group of
characters in the novel.

00:23:29.880 --> 00:23:30.990
And in crucial scenes,

00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:33.570
Malaquais draws out the
anxieties and emotions

00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:35.190
of Jewish language

00:23:35.190 --> 00:23:38.730
in direct opposition to its
portrayal by the far right

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:41.580
as racial contamination of France.

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:43.640
And here I'm gonna talk
about an example of Yiddish

00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:45.300
in the text.

00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:47.160
The reader first encounters

00:23:47.160 --> 00:23:49.770
the characters Pawel and Sonia Krantz,

00:23:49.770 --> 00:23:51.840
who are Jewish refugees from Poland,

00:23:51.840 --> 00:23:53.970
on the day before their arrest.

00:23:53.970 --> 00:23:56.910
They're in conversation
with Gustave Henri Hirsch,

00:23:56.910 --> 00:23:58.500
or Hirsch in English.

00:23:58.500 --> 00:24:01.560
Hirsch is a French Jew who
has lost a son in Dunkirk,

00:24:01.560 --> 00:24:05.400
and another son has been
taken prisoner by the Nazis.

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:09.360
His father is interned in the
Drancy camp, outside of Paris,

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:11.160
and he now sees himself as the last

00:24:11.160 --> 00:24:13.113
of the line of the French Hirsches.

00:24:14.040 --> 00:24:15.300
The narrator specifies

00:24:15.300 --> 00:24:17.040
that this group was speaking in French,

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:19.920
which is the only language
that Hirsch understands,

00:24:19.920 --> 00:24:22.980
but the conversation
unexpectedly turns to Yiddish,

00:24:22.980 --> 00:24:26.283
and it reveals anxieties
about Jewish bilingualism,

00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:33.680
in this case, bilingualism of
Yiddish and another language.

00:24:33.780 --> 00:24:38.780
Sonia Krantz's hold on her
sanity has been slipping

00:24:38.850 --> 00:24:41.850
over the course of the conversation,

00:24:41.850 --> 00:24:44.610
as she fears the worst for her family.

00:24:44.610 --> 00:24:48.300
And as the group is discussing
exit strategies and visas,

00:24:48.300 --> 00:24:50.490
Hirsch begins to laugh hysterically,

00:24:50.490 --> 00:24:52.740
and Sonia yells in his face and screams

00:24:52.740 --> 00:24:56.130
that she wants to know what he
knows and isn't telling her.

00:24:56.130 --> 00:24:59.430
And amid all the anger
and the tension and fear

00:24:59.430 --> 00:25:02.010
and Hirsch's laughter, they kiss.

00:25:02.010 --> 00:25:04.950
And Pawel, her husband, begins to curse,

00:25:04.950 --> 00:25:08.490
first in Polish, then in
German, then in Yiddish,

00:25:08.490 --> 00:25:11.550
and the Yiddish is actually represented

00:25:11.550 --> 00:25:15.750
in the text, interestingly,

00:25:15.750 --> 00:25:17.160
in French, not in Yiddish,

00:25:17.160 --> 00:25:19.380
but he's supposed to be speaking Yiddish,

00:25:19.380 --> 00:25:21.360
so it's an interesting
thing that happens here.

00:25:21.360 --> 00:25:24.240
And I have a couple of examples:
"A black year in yourguts."

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:25.860
And that's not a typo.

00:25:25.860 --> 00:25:28.923
In the text, the words
are all linked together.

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:34.110
This is Pawel speaking at first.

00:25:34.110 --> 00:25:36.540
And then, this scene
then indicates anxieties

00:25:36.540 --> 00:25:38.310
about Yiddish speech during the war

00:25:38.310 --> 00:25:41.940
because Hirsch is provoked by
Pawel's outburst in Yiddish,

00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:43.200
and he says he hears him

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:45.573
muttering his revolting incantations.

00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:49.080
Pawel is intoning psalms,

00:25:49.080 --> 00:25:52.920
and we find this long
reflection by Hirsch.

00:25:52.920 --> 00:25:55.410
It's actually free indirect discourse,

00:25:55.410 --> 00:25:58.410
so it's as if the narrator
is in the mind of Hirsch.

00:25:58.410 --> 00:26:01.500
And I'll read it: "It was
because of him," him being Pawel.

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:04.057
So Hirsch is thinking about
Pawel's use of Yiddish:

00:26:04.057 --> 00:26:06.510
"It was because of him that
France was no longer France,

00:26:06.510 --> 00:26:08.853
and life, no longer fit to be to live.

00:26:09.690 --> 00:26:11.340
Hirsch was not an anti-Semite.

00:26:11.340 --> 00:26:13.620
How could he be, feeling
so little that he was a Jew

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:15.870
so not at all Jewish?"

00:26:15.870 --> 00:26:18.030
And Hirsch keeps telling himself

00:26:18.030 --> 00:26:20.550
that he cannot be anti-Semitic.

00:26:20.550 --> 00:26:22.410
He obviously hates anti-Semites.

00:26:22.410 --> 00:26:25.300
And yet the sight of these
Eastern European Jews

00:26:26.249 --> 00:26:28.050
fills him with rage.

00:26:28.050 --> 00:26:31.080
He says, other than in
reference to the Dreyfus affair,

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:34.200
he'd never heard the word
juive, or Jew, in France

00:26:34.200 --> 00:26:36.873
until Eastern European Jews came in.

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:39.750
Hirsch doesn't understand Yiddish,

00:26:39.750 --> 00:26:42.330
but just hearing it does something to him,

00:26:42.330 --> 00:26:45.300
and what begins as this
terrible condemnation;

00:26:45.300 --> 00:26:46.680
he goes on and on;

00:26:46.680 --> 00:26:49.110
of Eastern European immigrant Jews

00:26:49.110 --> 00:26:50.610
ends with sympathy and love;

00:26:50.610 --> 00:26:52.680
for Hirsch soon sees that
he doesn't believe any

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:55.140
of the horrible things he's
thinking of the immigrants,

00:26:55.140 --> 00:26:58.440
and he realizes he's just
scared out of his mind.

00:26:58.440 --> 00:26:59.273
But this Yiddish

00:26:59.273 --> 00:27:02.070
and his perception of
its rhythm of incantation

00:27:02.070 --> 00:27:03.960
brings out his own anxieties

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:06.603
about his rejection from
French life as a Jew.

00:27:07.470 --> 00:27:11.340
Hirsch gives voice to the fear in response

00:27:11.340 --> 00:27:14.220
to anti-Semitic representations
of Jewish language

00:27:14.220 --> 00:27:18.273
as the central theme of their
exclusion from the nation.

00:27:20.670 --> 00:27:22.720
Another example is

00:27:24.690 --> 00:27:26.610
thinking about accent.

00:27:26.610 --> 00:27:27.990
I spoke about Yiddish in the book

00:27:27.990 --> 00:27:29.100
and now like to think

00:27:29.100 --> 00:27:32.760
about how Malaquais responds to accents

00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:34.620
in the anti-Semitic press,

00:27:34.620 --> 00:27:36.570
and I would say it's a form

00:27:36.570 --> 00:27:40.410
of reappropriation or
reclaiming these accents.

00:27:40.410 --> 00:27:42.570
He uses the same belittled speech

00:27:42.570 --> 00:27:45.483
but now against racial
discourse about accents.

00:27:46.710 --> 00:27:49.143
We have another character,
named Raya Bergmann.

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:53.400
You see in the first
line she's thinking...

00:27:53.400 --> 00:27:54.750
She just had a dental procedure.

00:27:54.750 --> 00:27:56.040
She's Jewish.

00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:58.590
We know that, and she's
had a dental procedure,

00:27:58.590 --> 00:28:03.000
and she is just speaking
with S's and Z's and sh's.

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:06.303
I'm giving you the, (coughs)
excuse me, English translation.

00:28:07.470 --> 00:28:09.243
Sorry, need water.

00:28:13.020 --> 00:28:16.263
But the French would be B's and D's.

00:28:18.180 --> 00:28:20.550
And so it's a joke because
she had a dental procedure,

00:28:20.550 --> 00:28:21.990
and that's why she's speaking in the way

00:28:21.990 --> 00:28:23.550
that you would normally associate

00:28:23.550 --> 00:28:26.190
with the stereotypes of Jewish voice,

00:28:26.190 --> 00:28:30.690
but actually, Malaquais revised the novel,

00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:32.850
which was originally published in 1947.

00:28:32.850 --> 00:28:35.910
He revised it in the '90s,
right before he died,

00:28:35.910 --> 00:28:39.150
for a republication,
and he changed the scene

00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:41.850
so that we first meet Raya

00:28:41.850 --> 00:28:43.110
and she's just speaking

00:28:43.110 --> 00:28:45.870
in this accent that would normally seem

00:28:45.870 --> 00:28:47.337
like the stereotypical Jewish accent.

00:28:47.337 --> 00:28:50.610
And I provided the quotes here.

00:28:50.610 --> 00:28:52.140
And then, as it goes on, you realize

00:28:52.140 --> 00:28:53.250
she's only speaking that way

00:28:53.250 --> 00:28:55.923
because she had a dental procedure.

00:28:57.960 --> 00:29:01.500
The dentist said that her good
old diction would come back,

00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:03.090
and you think, "Oh, she
probably doesn't have

00:29:03.090 --> 00:29:05.250
what this French dentist would think of

00:29:05.250 --> 00:29:06.690
as good Jewish diction."

00:29:06.690 --> 00:29:08.280
But then, you learn more about her,

00:29:08.280 --> 00:29:11.040
and you realize that she's
actually from Brittany.

00:29:11.040 --> 00:29:12.690
And so in this scene,

00:29:12.690 --> 00:29:15.600
Malaquais is playing with our assumptions

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:19.050
or breaking our assumptions
of Jewish accent

00:29:19.050 --> 00:29:21.000
to make a kind of joke about it here,

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:22.950
and he's disrupting a category

00:29:22.950 --> 00:29:26.133
that, at the time, left
no room for disruption.

00:29:27.930 --> 00:29:31.050
He's doing this in the face
of anti-Semitic writings

00:29:31.050 --> 00:29:33.030
in which language cannot surprise us

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:36.780
but instead is supposed to
be a defining aspect of race.

00:29:36.780 --> 00:29:39.000
These are just some
examples I'm giving you

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:42.134
of how Malaquais takes
anti-Semitic discourse

00:29:42.134 --> 00:29:43.500
about Jewish speech

00:29:43.500 --> 00:29:44.973
and turns it on its head.

00:29:47.880 --> 00:29:52.293
I'd like to finally go to
another author, Benjamin Fondane.

00:29:53.310 --> 00:29:56.190
There's a great photograph by Man Ray

00:29:56.190 --> 00:29:57.570
that I wanted to share.

00:29:57.570 --> 00:30:01.710
And I wanna look at his portrayal
of displacement and exile

00:30:01.710 --> 00:30:05.040
under the fear of arrest and
the threat of deportation.

00:30:05.040 --> 00:30:08.400
Fondane was a Romanian-born
poet and philosopher

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:11.310
who immigrated to France in 1923.

00:30:11.310 --> 00:30:12.810
He was already an active member

00:30:12.810 --> 00:30:15.300
of the Romanian literary
avant-garde in Bucharest

00:30:15.300 --> 00:30:17.190
before he emigrated.

00:30:17.190 --> 00:30:20.040
He became a French citizen in 1938

00:30:20.040 --> 00:30:22.470
and was mobilized into the French infantry

00:30:22.470 --> 00:30:25.350
and served in what has
been called the Phony War.

00:30:25.350 --> 00:30:29.460
He was taken prisoner by the
Nazis in the Loire region

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:32.910
in June 1940, just a few
days before the armistice.

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:34.110
After he escaped,

00:30:34.110 --> 00:30:36.240
he found himself on the roads in France

00:30:36.240 --> 00:30:39.390
during the civilian flight
that I described before.

00:30:39.390 --> 00:30:41.640
And then, he was hospitalized

00:30:41.640 --> 00:30:45.000
in the Val-de-Grace
military hospital in Paris

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:46.740
for appendicitis.

00:30:46.740 --> 00:30:50.460
He spent the occupation living
in semi hiding in Paris,

00:30:50.460 --> 00:30:52.470
where he refused to wear the yellow star,

00:30:52.470 --> 00:30:54.660
and he continued to write, for a time,

00:30:54.660 --> 00:30:57.450
in the literary journal "Cahiers du sud,"

00:30:57.450 --> 00:30:59.220
which was based in Marseille

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:00.630
and which helped him out

00:31:00.630 --> 00:31:04.140
when he needed to find a
place to live in Marseille.

00:31:04.140 --> 00:31:06.720
And I wanted to share a
really amazing postcard

00:31:06.720 --> 00:31:08.610
that he wrote to Jean Ballard,

00:31:08.610 --> 00:31:11.127
who was the editor of "Cahiers du sud."

00:31:12.780 --> 00:31:15.420
He wrote this in October 1940,

00:31:15.420 --> 00:31:18.180
and on the postcard, he
expressed his intention

00:31:18.180 --> 00:31:19.770
to continue publishing.

00:31:19.770 --> 00:31:21.570
Ballard was actually instrumental

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:25.560
in helping to get Fondane
citizenship before the war,

00:31:25.560 --> 00:31:29.400
and Fondane turns this
postcard into a creative text.

00:31:29.400 --> 00:31:31.440
It's the censored wartime card

00:31:31.440 --> 00:31:34.170
with simple fill-in-the-blank
lines used by soldiers

00:31:34.170 --> 00:31:36.630
to communicate briefly about their welfare

00:31:36.630 --> 00:31:38.100
with their families.

00:31:38.100 --> 00:31:41.547
But early on, you can
see (speaks in French),

00:31:45.990 --> 00:31:48.530
so you're supposed to be speaking about...

00:31:49.680 --> 00:31:51.990
These words mean killed,
prisoner, deceased,

00:31:51.990 --> 00:31:54.900
and here Fondane turns
it into a different text,

00:31:54.900 --> 00:31:58.041
and he says, (speaks in French)

00:31:58.041 --> 00:32:01.560
so "If 'Le Cahiers du
sud' have not been killed,

00:32:01.560 --> 00:32:05.430
I remain their prisoner;
philosophy, not deceased."

00:32:05.430 --> 00:32:09.810
So Fondane bends the censored
rigid words on the postcard

00:32:09.810 --> 00:32:11.280
into a literary text,

00:32:11.280 --> 00:32:13.620
and likewise, he would continue to write

00:32:13.620 --> 00:32:15.570
within the censored language of French

00:32:15.570 --> 00:32:18.990
to express his experience
under the occupation,

00:32:18.990 --> 00:32:21.603
writing a poetic vision of exile.

00:32:22.950 --> 00:32:25.590
Fondane is a particularly
interesting case for thinking

00:32:25.590 --> 00:32:28.950
about how authors changed
their approaches to writing

00:32:28.950 --> 00:32:31.530
and how they responded to
their position in France

00:32:31.530 --> 00:32:32.610
under the occupation,

00:32:32.610 --> 00:32:36.030
and I say that because he
revised most of his poetry;

00:32:36.030 --> 00:32:38.490
much of it, already
published during the war.

00:32:38.490 --> 00:32:40.740
And he left these revisions
behind with his wife,

00:32:40.740 --> 00:32:42.210
who survived him,

00:32:42.210 --> 00:32:44.310
and he reorganized his poems

00:32:44.310 --> 00:32:47.400
into a collection called
"Le mal des fantomes,"

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:49.800
which could be translated
as "The Ache of Phantoms"

00:32:49.800 --> 00:32:52.890
or maybe a play on words
of (speaks in French),

00:32:52.890 --> 00:32:53.970
which means homesickness,

00:32:53.970 --> 00:32:56.400
so maybe "Homesickness of Phantoms."

00:32:56.400 --> 00:32:58.230
And it was first published in the '60s

00:32:58.230 --> 00:33:01.023
and is only now really being
translated into English.

00:33:02.160 --> 00:33:03.870
The multiple manuscript versions

00:33:03.870 --> 00:33:06.180
of "Le mal des fantomes" are housed

00:33:06.180 --> 00:33:09.750
at the archive Bibliotheque
litteraire Jacques Doucet

00:33:09.750 --> 00:33:13.470
in Paris, and these archival
materials really bring to life

00:33:13.470 --> 00:33:16.500
the way Fondane was questioning
and reworking his ideas

00:33:16.500 --> 00:33:19.140
and thinking about exile, in particular.

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:20.520
And at this point,

00:33:20.520 --> 00:33:22.620
I would like to focus
on one very long poem

00:33:22.620 --> 00:33:25.597
that Fondant revised during the War,

00:33:25.597 --> 00:33:27.060
"Ulysse," or "Ulysses."

00:33:27.060 --> 00:33:30.390
And he began it in 1929
when he was in Argentina,

00:33:30.390 --> 00:33:32.760
working on a surrealist film project,

00:33:32.760 --> 00:33:35.313
but he finished and
published the poem in 1933.

00:33:36.547 --> 00:33:39.600
"Already in the interwar
version of the poem,

00:33:39.600 --> 00:33:43.680
Fondane creates a Jewish
Ulysses on a 20th-century voyage

00:33:43.680 --> 00:33:46.740
to America," "to the Americas,"
actually, "and beyond,

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:47.940
always in exile."

00:33:47.940 --> 00:33:50.070
And he's accompanied by immigrants

00:33:50.070 --> 00:33:52.140
who are displaced after World War I

00:33:52.140 --> 00:33:53.493
and in the wake of pogroms.

00:33:56.363 --> 00:34:00.030
Fondane marked up the
1933 edition of "Ulysse,"

00:34:00.030 --> 00:34:02.160
published by "Cahiers
du journal des poetes,"

00:34:02.160 --> 00:34:04.980
making revisions in the 1940s by hand

00:34:04.980 --> 00:34:06.600
on its published pages,

00:34:06.600 --> 00:34:10.110
writing out new pages and
typing out new sheets of poems.

00:34:10.110 --> 00:34:12.780
And this archival object
really shows the shifts

00:34:12.780 --> 00:34:14.040
in his writing.

00:34:14.040 --> 00:34:16.860
On the title page, he crossed
out the date of publication

00:34:16.860 --> 00:34:18.720
and changed it to 1944.

00:34:18.720 --> 00:34:20.880
He updated his list of published works.

00:34:20.880 --> 00:34:22.920
He crossed out the name of the publisher.

00:34:22.920 --> 00:34:27.270
And on a different page than from here

00:34:27.270 --> 00:34:28.920
that I don't have an image of,

00:34:28.920 --> 00:34:30.810
he creates his own invented press name

00:34:30.810 --> 00:34:34.650
called "Edition sans fin,"
or "No-End Editions,"

00:34:34.650 --> 00:34:37.050
as if to indicate that his
writing would be interrupted

00:34:37.050 --> 00:34:39.720
or that he found no sense of completion.

00:34:39.720 --> 00:34:42.330
As a Jewish emigre writer,
Fondane was now cut off

00:34:42.330 --> 00:34:44.520
from literary life in occupied Paris

00:34:44.520 --> 00:34:47.220
because it was illegal for him to publish.

00:34:47.220 --> 00:34:51.750
In his unpublished afterword to "Ulysse,"

00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:53.220
he explains his motivation

00:34:53.220 --> 00:34:56.010
to rewrite his poetry in the 1940s,

00:34:56.010 --> 00:34:57.390
and his current position in France

00:34:57.390 --> 00:34:59.677
is at the heart of this decision:

00:34:59.677 --> 00:35:02.407
"The second version of
'Ulysse,'" or "Ulysses,"

00:35:02.407 --> 00:35:04.890
"is being shaped day by
day; it is not yet finished;

00:35:04.890 --> 00:35:06.690
by a man who has just barely returned

00:35:06.690 --> 00:35:09.480
from a prisoner-of-war
camp and from the hospital

00:35:09.480 --> 00:35:11.730
and whom the police are
tracking at every intersection

00:35:11.730 --> 00:35:13.800
to throw him in a concentration camp."

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:15.487
And he actually crosses out

00:35:15.487 --> 00:35:18.300
"to throw him in a concentration camp,"

00:35:18.300 --> 00:35:22.140
and this erasure of the
disclosure of his ultimate fear

00:35:22.140 --> 00:35:24.930
or the ultimate fear of being
sent to a concentration camp

00:35:24.930 --> 00:35:26.910
is a kind of self-censorship

00:35:26.910 --> 00:35:29.520
on a note that would
never even be published,

00:35:29.520 --> 00:35:32.430
and in fact, he wonders if
his poem will ever be finished

00:35:32.430 --> 00:35:33.993
or if he'll be interrupted.

00:35:35.340 --> 00:35:36.870
An afterword he typed up

00:35:36.870 --> 00:35:40.620
for the final version of the
poem "Exode," "The Exodus,"

00:35:40.620 --> 00:35:43.140
might further explain his situation.

00:35:43.140 --> 00:35:45.930
He wrote that, although he
could not publish at the time,

00:35:45.930 --> 00:35:48.663
books search for friends
rather than a public.

00:35:52.260 --> 00:35:54.300
He ends it with a line that's often used

00:35:54.300 --> 00:35:56.520
in resistance pamphlets,

00:35:56.520 --> 00:35:59.340
which is he asks the
reader to take the trouble

00:35:59.340 --> 00:36:03.030
to recopy or have recopied his text.

00:36:03.030 --> 00:36:05.100
Since he could no longer publish openly

00:36:05.100 --> 00:36:06.480
as he had done before the war,

00:36:06.480 --> 00:36:08.913
Fondane creates his own
edition of his poem,

00:36:11.730 --> 00:36:16.390
and I can show one
particularly telling change

00:36:17.559 --> 00:36:21.030
that has been studied before,
especially by Monique Jutrin,

00:36:21.030 --> 00:36:22.730
who's written a lot about Fondane.

00:36:23.820 --> 00:36:25.447
In 1933, he has a line:

00:36:25.447 --> 00:36:27.870
"a Jew naturally and yet Ulysses."

00:36:27.870 --> 00:36:29.307
And then, he changed it in the '40s

00:36:29.307 --> 00:36:32.040
to "a Jew, naturally,
you were a Jew, Ulysses."

00:36:32.040 --> 00:36:35.400
And so Jewishness is
no longer a coincidence

00:36:35.400 --> 00:36:37.440
but rather a natural part of the condition

00:36:37.440 --> 00:36:39.033
of traveling and displacement.

00:36:40.980 --> 00:36:43.560
Maybe in the few moments we have...

00:36:43.560 --> 00:36:45.180
I'm sorry I'm a couple of minutes over,

00:36:45.180 --> 00:36:48.690
but I wanted to look
at the poem "L'Exode."

00:36:48.690 --> 00:36:53.040
And it's a long poem that's a
cycle of dramatic dialogues,

00:36:53.040 --> 00:36:55.680
and he added a whole
section, during the war,

00:36:55.680 --> 00:36:59.523
about the June 1940 defeat
and civilian flight.

00:37:00.630 --> 00:37:05.520
The poem is about these cycles
of exile throughout history,

00:37:05.520 --> 00:37:07.860
and he represents each with a river,

00:37:07.860 --> 00:37:10.350
the Nile for Jewish enslavement in Egypt;

00:37:10.350 --> 00:37:13.170
the rivers of Babylon,
or the shores of Babylon,

00:37:13.170 --> 00:37:15.030
for the destruction of the Second Temple

00:37:15.030 --> 00:37:17.160
and the exile from Jerusalem;

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:21.270
and finally, the Seine for
the June 1940 civilian flight.

00:37:21.270 --> 00:37:25.500
And the poem takes its central
imagery from Psalm 1:37

00:37:25.500 --> 00:37:28.290
about the lamentation of the Jewish people

00:37:28.290 --> 00:37:29.700
after the siege of Jerusalem.

00:37:29.700 --> 00:37:33.303
I won't read it, but this
repeats throughout the poem,

00:37:34.470 --> 00:37:36.240
and by using the psalm,

00:37:36.240 --> 00:37:38.790
Fondane assimilates the 1940 Exode

00:37:38.790 --> 00:37:41.400
into a recurring history of exile.

00:37:41.400 --> 00:37:44.100
And in so doing, he compares
the exile of the Jews

00:37:44.100 --> 00:37:47.880
after the destruction of the
Second Temple in Jerusalem

00:37:47.880 --> 00:37:49.350
to the Exodus from Paris

00:37:49.350 --> 00:37:52.300
and a kind of destruction of a lost Zion

00:37:53.190 --> 00:37:57.540
and a paradise for
Jewish immigrant authors.

00:37:57.540 --> 00:38:00.000
There's another section
that makes this quite clear.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:04.147
He transforms the famous
line from Psalm 1:37:

00:38:04.147 --> 00:38:05.730
"If I forget you, oh Jerusalem."

00:38:05.730 --> 00:38:07.680
He says, "We were leaving Paris behind us.

00:38:07.680 --> 00:38:10.167
Ah, if ever I forget you, Jerusalem."

00:38:11.010 --> 00:38:14.430
And Fondane also addresses

00:38:14.430 --> 00:38:17.820
a linguistic pain associated with exile,

00:38:17.820 --> 00:38:20.070
and that's forgetting your own language

00:38:20.070 --> 00:38:22.470
and being displaced in a new language.

00:38:22.470 --> 00:38:25.260
At the moment of leaving Paris/Jerusalem,

00:38:25.260 --> 00:38:28.140
the speaker exclaims
that he gets on his knees

00:38:28.140 --> 00:38:29.400
and sobs in this language

00:38:29.400 --> 00:38:31.500
that he forgets but that he remembers.

00:38:31.500 --> 00:38:35.880
And you see he has
transliterated Hebrew here,

00:38:35.880 --> 00:38:38.160
and this is the words
from the Shema prayer,

00:38:38.160 --> 00:38:41.790
the most fundamentally
important prayer for Jews,

00:38:41.790 --> 00:38:43.680
a declaration of unity with God.

00:38:43.680 --> 00:38:46.470
And so Hebrew here is
transliterated in the text,

00:38:46.470 --> 00:38:48.570
and it's interesting to
note he makes a mistake,

00:38:48.570 --> 00:38:50.220
actually, in the Hebrew,

00:38:50.220 --> 00:38:54.840
and it's kind of this memory
of the prayer for him.

00:38:54.840 --> 00:38:58.450
So throughout the poem, Fondane expresses

00:38:59.940 --> 00:39:02.910
the complexity of losing
France after the defeat,

00:39:02.910 --> 00:39:04.980
of losing his position in France,

00:39:04.980 --> 00:39:06.483
a double loss of home,

00:39:07.320 --> 00:39:11.880
as a Jewish immigrant and as a writer

00:39:11.880 --> 00:39:14.310
'cause he's lost his position
as a writer in France,

00:39:14.310 --> 00:39:16.010
or his ability to publish anymore.

00:39:16.980 --> 00:39:20.010
I'll just come to a conclusion now

00:39:20.010 --> 00:39:22.860
and say a little bit what
happened to these two writers.

00:39:22.860 --> 00:39:27.860
Fondane was very tragically deported

00:39:28.260 --> 00:39:31.710
to Auschwitz from Drancy
in 1944, and he died

00:39:31.710 --> 00:39:35.610
just a few weeks after the
liberation of France that year.

00:39:35.610 --> 00:39:39.453
His archives are, as I said, in Paris.

00:39:41.100 --> 00:39:45.180
You can look at them in a room
that overlooks the Pantheon,

00:39:45.180 --> 00:39:48.210
a secular temple to the great
men and women of France.

00:39:48.210 --> 00:39:51.210
And there's actually a
plaque in the Pantheon

00:39:51.210 --> 00:39:52.770
about writers who died in the war,

00:39:52.770 --> 00:39:54.123
and his name is included.

00:39:55.487 --> 00:39:58.170
And as I mentioned, Malaquais
was able to get to Mexico,

00:39:58.170 --> 00:39:59.850
and he eventually came to America

00:39:59.850 --> 00:40:02.010
and became an American citizen.

00:40:02.010 --> 00:40:03.210
He had a Guggenheim Fellowship

00:40:03.210 --> 00:40:06.780
and did a Ph.D. in comparative
literature, I believe.

00:40:06.780 --> 00:40:07.860
So these two writers had

00:40:07.860 --> 00:40:10.770
completely different paths and politics.

00:40:10.770 --> 00:40:12.390
They didn't know each other,

00:40:12.390 --> 00:40:14.190
but they fill a gap

00:40:14.190 --> 00:40:17.220
in what Jewish writers were
thinking about and writing about

00:40:17.220 --> 00:40:19.020
during the war itself.

00:40:19.020 --> 00:40:20.670
I didn't have so much of a chance

00:40:20.670 --> 00:40:24.450
to engage with Fondane's
multilingualism in his poetry,

00:40:24.450 --> 00:40:27.000
but both authors were very interested

00:40:27.000 --> 00:40:30.600
in French language and accent

00:40:30.600 --> 00:40:32.880
and transliteration of other languages,

00:40:32.880 --> 00:40:34.950
how to break down the barrier

00:40:34.950 --> 00:40:37.203
between French and other languages.

00:40:38.430 --> 00:40:40.440
They were both very
engaged with revisions.

00:40:40.440 --> 00:40:44.670
As I said, Malaquais revised
his book just before his death,

00:40:44.670 --> 00:40:48.000
so he was thinking about this
into the end of his life,

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:51.453
and Fondane revised his
poetry during the war.

00:40:55.650 --> 00:40:58.350
I'm happy to continue the
conversation in Q and A,

00:40:58.350 --> 00:40:59.703
and thank you so much.

00:41:03.000 --> 00:41:04.830
- Julia, thank you.

00:41:04.830 --> 00:41:08.280
That was such a double pleasure,

00:41:08.280 --> 00:41:10.890
your sense of the material world,

00:41:10.890 --> 00:41:15.090
both the materiality of language, words,

00:41:15.090 --> 00:41:18.840
and also these amazing archival documents

00:41:18.840 --> 00:41:20.670
that you put on the PowerPoint.

00:41:20.670 --> 00:41:24.093
The postcard that he does a detournement,

00:41:25.230 --> 00:41:27.630
recreates it as a literary
text, that's amazing.

00:41:27.630 --> 00:41:29.700
I've never seen anything like that.

00:41:29.700 --> 00:41:32.550
They're both very appealing characters.

00:41:32.550 --> 00:41:33.990
- Yeah, yeah.

00:41:33.990 --> 00:41:36.617
- I guess my first question
for you is about accents

00:41:36.617 --> 00:41:40.890
'cause I think your
analysis is so powerful.

00:41:40.890 --> 00:41:42.390
One of the things I was thinking,

00:41:42.390 --> 00:41:46.260
looking at the representations
of the Jewish accent,

00:41:46.260 --> 00:41:49.770
is there was a problem in occupied France,

00:41:49.770 --> 00:41:52.500
which is that the Yiddish accent sounds

00:41:52.500 --> 00:41:55.080
just like the German accent.

00:41:55.080 --> 00:41:56.820
So presumably, all these Nazis

00:41:56.820 --> 00:42:00.290
would also have made those same...

00:42:01.770 --> 00:42:04.620
You woulda been imitated
exactly the same way

00:42:04.620 --> 00:42:06.873
so that this French nationalism,

00:42:09.660 --> 00:42:10.493
on the right,

00:42:10.493 --> 00:42:14.283
had to embrace Naziism and still mock.

00:42:17.220 --> 00:42:20.370
- And I wonder if that's the reason

00:42:20.370 --> 00:42:24.210
why Rebatet refers to
it as Yiddish accents,

00:42:24.210 --> 00:42:27.030
because, actually, most of
these people in the cafes,

00:42:27.030 --> 00:42:28.950
they're not speaking Yiddish.

00:42:28.950 --> 00:42:31.170
They're speaking German with
each other, a lot of them.

00:42:31.170 --> 00:42:33.810
And so maybe that's why he has to make it

00:42:33.810 --> 00:42:34.680
that they're speaking Yiddish,

00:42:34.680 --> 00:42:37.563
that they can't be speaking German.

00:42:38.820 --> 00:42:42.213
And Malaquais does funny things, too.

00:42:43.710 --> 00:42:46.230
He shows that all of these refugees

00:42:46.230 --> 00:42:47.910
have all kinds of different accents,

00:42:47.910 --> 00:42:51.030
but he also shows that
the Germans have accents.

00:42:51.030 --> 00:42:56.030
He also shows that there are
all these Persian French Jews

00:42:56.160 --> 00:42:59.460
and they're confronting
the Southern French accent.

00:42:59.460 --> 00:43:01.320
So he's showing how
everyone has an accent.

00:43:01.320 --> 00:43:02.820
There isn't this native French accent.

00:43:02.820 --> 00:43:06.030
- Well, yeah, I think there's
something very important

00:43:06.030 --> 00:43:08.670
in what you're saying about...

00:43:08.670 --> 00:43:10.612
We need to reconceive of accents.

00:43:10.612 --> 00:43:14.880
You and I know that our field
was absolutely reactionary

00:43:14.880 --> 00:43:16.050
on the question of accents.

00:43:16.050 --> 00:43:18.300
There was only one way to speak French.

00:43:18.300 --> 00:43:19.133
- [Julia] Yeah.

00:43:19.133 --> 00:43:22.860
- And having the wrong
accent could deny you tenure.

00:43:22.860 --> 00:43:26.334
I'm exaggerating, but there
was something about that,

00:43:26.334 --> 00:43:29.520
and the other thing you
said I thought was very wise

00:43:29.520 --> 00:43:32.163
was that, for the anti-Semites,

00:43:33.510 --> 00:43:35.700
the question of accent,

00:43:35.700 --> 00:43:37.950
there was only one kind of accent.

00:43:37.950 --> 00:43:41.790
It meant that language was
completely predictable.

00:43:41.790 --> 00:43:45.180
- Right, right, that it was just

00:43:45.180 --> 00:43:47.660
racially defined somehow and

00:43:47.660 --> 00:43:50.910
you were born a certain way.
- That it was like a template.

00:43:50.910 --> 00:43:54.360
You'd either say it with
the G or the P or whatever,

00:43:54.360 --> 00:43:56.591
or you'd say it in the right way.

00:43:56.591 --> 00:43:57.440
- Right, right.

00:43:57.440 --> 00:43:59.100
- And I guess what you're showing

00:43:59.100 --> 00:44:00.900
about Malaquais and Fondane

00:44:00.900 --> 00:44:04.860
is that they really made
language unpredictable

00:44:04.860 --> 00:44:06.153
in every way.

00:44:08.340 --> 00:44:09.990
- Yeah, thank you.

00:44:09.990 --> 00:44:13.350
Yes, Fondane, in his poem,

00:44:13.350 --> 00:44:15.120
also shows that there isn't just

00:44:15.120 --> 00:44:19.293
this one monolingual language.

00:44:20.850 --> 00:44:22.200
There are other parts of his poems

00:44:22.200 --> 00:44:25.500
where he does all kinds of
interesting thing with language.

00:44:25.500 --> 00:44:28.890
The speaker says, "He's
speaking my language,

00:44:28.890 --> 00:44:30.090
but I don't understand it,"

00:44:30.090 --> 00:44:32.370
or other people are speaking his language,

00:44:32.370 --> 00:44:33.267
and he doesn't understand it.

00:44:33.267 --> 00:44:38.267
And this breakdown of
what monolingualism is

00:44:38.280 --> 00:44:41.730
as Derrida would do many years later.

00:44:41.730 --> 00:44:43.680
But already, in his poems, I think,

00:44:43.680 --> 00:44:47.430
he's trying to get away
from the idea of French

00:44:47.430 --> 00:44:50.580
as this one monolingual language

00:44:50.580 --> 00:44:53.400
with these barriers
that keep everyone out.

00:44:53.400 --> 00:44:56.130
- French has gotten more
interesting, for sure.

00:44:56.130 --> 00:44:57.180
I was struck

00:44:57.180 --> 00:45:01.320
when you quoted the translations
from "Planete sans visa"

00:45:01.320 --> 00:45:04.110
that they were using
English for the Yiddish

00:45:04.110 --> 00:45:08.010
but just changing the spelling a little

00:45:08.010 --> 00:45:09.150
to make it non-standard.

00:45:09.150 --> 00:45:12.510
So in 1947 at Doubleday in New York,

00:45:12.510 --> 00:45:14.510
they didn't wanna reproduce Yiddish words

00:45:14.510 --> 00:45:16.620
in an English-language text?

00:45:16.620 --> 00:45:18.240
- Well, actually-
- Did they in French?

00:45:18.240 --> 00:45:19.500
Did they in the French?

00:45:19.500 --> 00:45:21.333
- No, in French, also, he doesn't.

00:45:24.630 --> 00:45:28.350
No, it's really interesting

00:45:28.350 --> 00:45:32.897
because both he, Jean Malaquais,

00:45:32.897 --> 00:45:36.180
and Romain Gary do this,
where they have characters...

00:45:36.180 --> 00:45:38.700
It's just in French, but
you're meant to understand

00:45:38.700 --> 00:45:40.470
that they're not speaking French,

00:45:40.470 --> 00:45:42.060
and it's really interesting to think

00:45:42.060 --> 00:45:46.263
about how they represent other languages.

00:45:47.416 --> 00:45:52.410
And I don't know if it's that Malaquais...

00:45:52.410 --> 00:45:55.530
Perhaps Malaquais really
wouldn't have fully been able

00:45:55.530 --> 00:45:56.850
to write that in Yiddish.

00:45:56.850 --> 00:46:00.450
And so it's interesting to
think about what it means.

00:46:00.450 --> 00:46:03.240
This is something that
more-contemporary writers in France

00:46:03.240 --> 00:46:04.710
have talked about,

00:46:04.710 --> 00:46:06.393
writing in Yiddish in French.

00:46:09.360 --> 00:46:11.880
Also, actually, a lot of
different contemporary writers

00:46:11.880 --> 00:46:14.253
have talked about this
idea in America also.

00:46:15.300 --> 00:46:17.400
- It's how you signify another language

00:46:17.400 --> 00:46:18.960
within the language, I guess.

00:46:18.960 --> 00:46:22.127
Isn't there an expression
like (speaks in French)

00:46:22.127 --> 00:46:24.273
or (speaks in French)

00:46:24.273 --> 00:46:26.062
being in a footnote?
- Yes, yes, yes, yeah.

00:46:26.062 --> 00:46:29.910
Yeah, yeah, that's good.

00:46:29.910 --> 00:46:33.990
- We have a few more minutes
if there are some questions.

00:46:33.990 --> 00:46:35.400
If there aren't more questions,

00:46:35.400 --> 00:46:37.834
you and I could go on talking forever.

00:46:37.834 --> 00:46:41.070
(Julia chuckles)

00:46:41.070 --> 00:46:42.210
Lemme ask you a question

00:46:42.210 --> 00:46:44.580
really on behalf of one of our students.

00:46:44.580 --> 00:46:47.670
who's starting to work on
Malaquais and Norman Mailer,

00:46:47.670 --> 00:46:50.520
because that was a very
enabling friendship

00:46:50.520 --> 00:46:52.620
for both of them really,

00:46:52.620 --> 00:46:55.710
the American and this guy who had...

00:46:55.710 --> 00:46:57.240
I guess it was Varian Fry

00:46:57.240 --> 00:47:00.018
who got him to Mexico and to the U.S.

00:47:00.018 --> 00:47:02.769
Is that who he got-
- Yeah, that's right.

00:47:02.769 --> 00:47:05.970
- And what do you think
Norman Mailer learned

00:47:05.970 --> 00:47:09.030
from Malaquais's multilingualism?

00:47:09.030 --> 00:47:10.950
Certainly, we don't associate Mailer

00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:13.290
with that kind of language play.

00:47:13.290 --> 00:47:14.733
- Yeah, that's interesting;

00:47:15.990 --> 00:47:18.963
I don't know if I have
a really good answer;

00:47:23.850 --> 00:47:28.680
because Malaquais translated
a text by Norman Mailer.

00:47:28.680 --> 00:47:33.680
But I have seen one film discussion

00:47:34.200 --> 00:47:36.060
between the two authors,

00:47:36.060 --> 00:47:39.120
and they were especially
talking about politics

00:47:39.120 --> 00:47:40.140
because Malaquais...

00:47:40.140 --> 00:47:41.370
I didn't have a chance
to really get into this,

00:47:41.370 --> 00:47:43.713
but he was a very committed Trotskyist.

00:47:45.060 --> 00:47:47.007
- Mexico was the right place for him.

00:47:47.007 --> 00:47:49.753
(Alice laughs)
- Right, yeah. (laughs)

00:47:50.880 --> 00:47:53.913
They were talking a lot
about that, and Malaquais

00:47:57.990 --> 00:48:00.730
was saying how he never

00:48:02.550 --> 00:48:04.080
compromised himself in any way

00:48:04.080 --> 00:48:07.320
and he was very staunch in his support.

00:48:07.320 --> 00:48:09.090
What stands out to me more

00:48:09.090 --> 00:48:11.910
is their conversation
about politics, I must say,

00:48:11.910 --> 00:48:13.280
but it is really interesting

00:48:13.280 --> 00:48:15.319
to think about the
connection between these two.

00:48:15.319 --> 00:48:16.890
- (indistinct) certainly think of Mailer

00:48:16.890 --> 00:48:18.630
as a super New Yorker,

00:48:18.630 --> 00:48:21.360
and we forget that he was ever...

00:48:21.360 --> 00:48:23.370
I think he was in France
for a year, wasn't he,

00:48:23.370 --> 00:48:25.530
on the G.I. Bill?

00:48:25.530 --> 00:48:28.470
- Oh, yes, I think so, yeah.

00:48:28.470 --> 00:48:29.520
I'm not so familiar.

00:48:29.520 --> 00:48:31.860
- Yeah, I'm sorry, I'm taking
you away from your topic.

00:48:31.860 --> 00:48:35.580
- No, no, not at all, but I
know that there are letters

00:48:35.580 --> 00:48:38.640
with them figuring out places to stay

00:48:38.640 --> 00:48:41.269
in France or vacation apartment in Paris.

00:48:41.269 --> 00:48:43.274
(Alice laughs)

00:48:43.274 --> 00:48:44.107
- That's (indistinct).

00:48:44.107 --> 00:48:47.460
- It's such an unexpected connection,

00:48:47.460 --> 00:48:48.810
Norman Mailer and Malaquais.

00:48:48.810 --> 00:48:51.660
- Can I ask you a little
more about translation?

00:48:51.660 --> 00:48:54.510
Malaquais translated himself?

00:48:54.510 --> 00:48:56.040
- No, he-

00:48:56.040 --> 00:48:57.450
- [Alice] Oh, Peter Grant translated-

00:48:57.450 --> 00:48:59.400
- Peter Grant translated him,

00:48:59.400 --> 00:49:01.440
but there was actually a different person

00:49:01.440 --> 00:49:03.180
who was supposed to,
originally, translate him.

00:49:03.180 --> 00:49:06.030
And there's a really
extensive correspondence

00:49:06.030 --> 00:49:08.430
between him and this
translator, and you see

00:49:08.430 --> 00:49:11.070
that Malaquais had a really
amazing grasp on English.

00:49:11.070 --> 00:49:13.140
He was a really talented polyglot.

00:49:13.140 --> 00:49:13.973
He knew many languages.

00:49:13.973 --> 00:49:15.960
- So he must have been very
involved in the translation.

00:49:15.960 --> 00:49:18.486
- He was, and it's interesting,

00:49:18.486 --> 00:49:22.620
the things he specifies to the translator.

00:49:22.620 --> 00:49:25.050
His archives are at
the Harry Ransom Center

00:49:25.050 --> 00:49:26.693
and very accessible there.
- Oh, that's fantastic.

00:49:26.693 --> 00:49:28.980
There's been all this work done recently

00:49:28.980 --> 00:49:30.750
on the lives of translators

00:49:30.750 --> 00:49:33.180
and on translator-author relationships,

00:49:33.180 --> 00:49:35.670
and this sounds like,
really, a good archive

00:49:35.670 --> 00:49:37.140
for people working on that.

00:49:37.140 --> 00:49:40.290
- Definitely, it's a really
interesting correspondence,

00:49:40.290 --> 00:49:41.123
yeah.

00:49:41.123 --> 00:49:43.320
- Yeah, somebody's asking

00:49:43.320 --> 00:49:47.153
if the conversation is going to be taped,

00:49:48.390 --> 00:49:52.200
and I think Inessa said
that it was gonna be taped.

00:49:52.200 --> 00:49:54.360
Lemme see what she's answering.

00:49:54.360 --> 00:49:56.850
Yes, in a couple of weeks,

00:49:56.850 --> 00:50:01.850
people will be able to
listen to Julia's lecture

00:50:02.160 --> 00:50:04.590
on a recording,

00:50:04.590 --> 00:50:08.850
which is great because there
was so much rich textual detail

00:50:08.850 --> 00:50:11.970
and ways for us to go back
and reread these two writers.

00:50:11.970 --> 00:50:14.970
So I just wanna thank you very much.

00:50:14.970 --> 00:50:16.110
What a pleasure.
- Thank you so much

00:50:16.110 --> 00:50:19.083
for the invitation. (chuckles)

